@Editor Help
The editor menus can be operated by using the arrows up/down and the
page up and page down keys, or using tab; push enter to activate the
selected menu item. Some letters are highlighted in green; you can
push ALT and the highlighted to select it. Some items allow entering
numbers or text; you can push CTRL+U to erase the number/text.

You can push ALT+? on almost any screen to display context-sensitive
online help for that screen.

Remember to save before you quit; otherwise your changes are lost.

The main screen of the editor contains the following submenus:
!-blist;Board list
!-slist;Screen list
!-stvar;Status variables
!-element;Elements
!-appmap;Appearance mapping
!-anima;Animations
!-numform;Numeric formats
!-edithelp;Help lumps
!global;Global script
!-sclib;Script library
!-editgr;Graphics
!-items;Items/inventory
!more;More

:global
The global script can be shared by any board that does not have the
"suppress global scripts" flag.

:more
The "More" submenu contains:

þ Auto pause: If selected, then the game is automatically paused when it
starts (you can still push the pause key to toggle it, whether or not this
is selected). While it is paused, a turn is only advanced when a key is
pushed, and otherwise does nothing.

þ 40 columns: If selected, then the display during the game will use 40
columns; if not selected, then it will use 80 columns. Some built-in menus
will always use 80 columns regardless of this. Note that if it is set to 40
columns then only the first 40 columns of the screen are used and the other
40 columns will not be displayed.

þ Joystick configuration: Edits the mapping of joystick buttons to the
commands within the game world.
!-editjoy;Joystick configuration editor

þ Default setting override: Allows overriding the settings specified in the
user configuration file. The user configuration file can contain a option to
prevent the default setting override from working, so it is not guaranteed
to use the override settings specified in the world file.
!-override;Default setting override editor

þ Advanced: Advanced functions which you might not need, such as editing
mandatory and optional OID sets directly.
!-editadv;Advanced editor

